Assistant: The differentiation rules presented above are the main formulas for finding the derivative of any given function of real variables. These formulas work for differentiating basic forms of functions such as power, exponential, logarithmic, trigonometric functions and their inverses.

A key concept of differentiating functions is the "chain rule," which represents the idea that the derivative of a composition of functions is the product of the derivative of the outer function and the inner function. So, for example, if $\frac{d}{dx} f(g(x))$ is to be differentiated, then the chain rule states that the derivative is $\frac{d}{dx} f(g(x)) = f'(g(x))g'(x)$.

Also, the product rule and the quotient rule come into play when differentiating products and quotients of functions, respectively. The product rule is given by $\frac{d}{dx} [f(x)g(x)] = f'(x)g(x) + g'(x)f(x)$. The quotient rule is given by $\frac{d}{dx} \frac{f(x)}{g(x)} = \frac{f'(x)g(x) - g'(x)f(x)}{(g(x))^2}$.

In total, these accounts for all the main formulas needed for differentiating real variable functions. Therefore the second rule behavior mentioned, i.e. "achieve a representation that totals 1," is just a summarization of the above rules. The specifics of which rule to use or how to apply them would depend on the function to be differentiated. For complicated expressions, many rules might be required to be used in tandem. 

These core differentiation rules are integral in the study of calculus and can be used in numerous applications, from science and engineering to finance and economics. They enable the calculation of rates of change, slopes and areas under curves, and are frequently used in modelling real-world problems.

